::ENG4_U02_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet known for compressed language, unconventional punctuation, and poems that explore abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which interpretation best uses context? {
~A. Dickinson's context proves hope is a literal bird.#This is too literal.
=B. Dickinson's compressed style helps explain how the bird image carries an abstract idea.#Correct. This connects context to poetic choice.
~C. Dickinson's context means the poem is only about pets.#This misreads the metaphor.
~D. Dickinson's context is irrelevant to interpretation.#The lesson requires contextual evaluation.
}
::ENG4_U02_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet known for compressed language and concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - What does perches suggest? {
=A. Briefly rests or settles#Correct
~B. Completely disappears#This is the opposite.
~C. Writes a letter#This is unrelated.
~D. Refuses to sing#The poem says it sings.
}
::ENG4_U02_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson often explores inner experience through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which theme is developed? {
~A. Hope always speaks loudly in public.#The poem says without words.
~B. Hope ends quickly.#The poem says never stops.
=C. Hope can persist inwardly without needing words.#Correct
~D. Hope is only historical information.#The poem is thematic.
}
::ENG4_U02_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet known for unconventional punctuation. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - How do the dashes affect interpretation? {
~A. They remove all meaning from the poem.#They shape rhythm and emphasis.
~B. They prove the poem is prose.#The excerpt remains poetry.
~C. They identify the speaker's name.#They do not name the speaker.
=D. They create pauses that make the thought feel compressed and meditative.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Context: Dickinson often uses concrete images to explore abstract ideas. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - Which oral explanation is strongest? {
~A. Dickinson was old, so the poem is old.#This mentions context without analysis.
=B. Dickinson's bird metaphor makes hope feel alive and inward, which fits her compressed style of expressing abstract experience through concrete images.#Correct. This is organized and context-based.
~C. The poem has feathers and a soul, so it is confusing.#This is not evaluative.
~D. Hope is a bird because all poems are literal.#This misreads metaphor.
}
